commentBody:
@SP

I think the theme was worthy, but the puzzle should have run on a Tuesday.

I don't even play Scrabble, and it was still way too easy.

@Twangster
It was there yesterday, too, and several of us commented on it.  It seems to appear a bit different for different people, but it's annoying however it occurs.

Maybe some day someone will figure out that they ought to un-break it...  I suspect we'll be living with it for a while, though.  These are the people who took away some of the Sunday extra puzzles a few years ago because they were "technically too difficult" to keep doing -- even though some were pdfs.

@Twangster
What are you all talking about?  Looks the same to me (MacBook Air (Tahoe 26.6.2)

⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️

@Twangster
I'm not having any trouble.  Mine works like normal on my
iPad

@Andrew
Not possible. Standard grid is 15×15 = 225 squares. Standard Scrabble tile set is 100 tiles. 
You'd have such an empty grid you'd be listening for the flutter of phantom wings behind you and wondering if you packed some garlic.  🦇

@Alex

Maybe I’m just behind the times here, then. But every definition in Merriam Webster qualifies hate with “intense”, “extreme” and “strong” which is certainly not my attitude to the puzzle. I might just agree that I “hate” (as in strongly dislike) that it ran on a Thursday., but even that’s a far cry from hating the puzzle.

The fact that it’s possible we can’t distinguish hate from reasoned criticism in modern times might explain a lot about the divisions in this country.

@Alexia

And you are absolutely right about that. I’ve been preaching that for a long time. I just wanted to be clear there is a difference between constructive criticism and disrespect or lack of recognition. If you meant people actually saying “I hate this”, I saw very few reviews with that attitude today, as I expected, even among its most vehement detractors—compare that to if this had been a very tricky Thursday or a full rebus. (But of course, people have a right to that sentiment as well if they want).

So, I’m sorry if I misunderstood or diminished your post, I didn’t mean to, just wanted to make the difference clear.

@SP

When was the last time we agreed to such a huge extent as today? 🤜🏾🤛🏾

I'll try your puzzle later - it sounds like I might be able to complete it for once 😃

@SP
I entered TREAD with confidence...
And later, I didn't think as I entered LEACH at 7A (nearing the end of my solve)...though 9D quickly disabused me of that idea.  Duh.

@SP

Of these, "Go to the dogs" (Zejść na psy, literally "descend to the dogs") is the only idiom also known in Polish. I have no idea what the bat thing might mean. The other two I've learned over the years.

Probably none of your puzzles would ever be up my alley - yours and my trivia pools barely overlap, and with how much misdirection there is in your clues (which is a good thing, mind you, generally speaking), I end up unable to make progress for lack of crosses. Saturday-level misdirection all over with almost no trivial gimmes will always defeat me. But that's on me, obviously. Experienced American solvers seem to love your puzzles, and I get why they would. I'm just the wrong target audience for you.

@Francis
I see your point. Maybe escaper is one in the process of escaping, escapee has accomplished it. Or should that be escaped? The escaped lived in Bolivia. I sure can't think of a similar word that ends in EE that isn't a recipient of some sort.

Then there are the words that come straight from French: soirée, fiancée. Escapee in French could be fugitif(ive) or interestingly évadé or évadée. Évadée would only refer to a female escapee. (Same with fiancée, only for a woman. A man would be fiancé.)

@Mike R
Would have to be the very first word played.
(And would be extremely impressive.)

@ImmodestyBlaise

For what it’s worth, in the iOS version, it accepted “BLANK” in both squares using the Rebus key. I didn’t think to try entering nothing at all.  

Mark

