My paternal grandfather was born in Italy, and I didn't move away from NY until 1990.  I guess I was just fortunate that no one in my circle of aquaintances said it.

@Chris Here's a sentence:

"I scored 72 points in Scrabble by placing the word ZED with the Z on a triple-word square to the left of an A to form the word ZA."

I played Scrabble enough to learn that ZA is an acceptable word. Never heard it used elsewhere.

@Chris

We're going to order a ZA' 'ZA for dinner.

(A pizza with za'atar)

@Joe
I’m a Gen-X Brooklyn Italian and not once did I hear “za” used there. 

I’ve heard it’s a New Haven thing.

@Times Rita
