They can't fly, and that didn't either.

@Nancy

I couldn't agree more.  It feels as if you wrote my comment for me.

It's not snobbish to find a puzzle seriously wanting!

@B
The opinion expressed was not snobbish, but rather the tone in which the opinion was expressed was deemed snobbish.
But NYC Nancy's commentary is pretty much always toned that way. (IMO.)

@Charles Peterson

Agreed.  That was classic "Nancy being Nancy," and it makes for interesting reading every day.

(I loved her rant yesterday about BALLOON POP.  I know she's not likely a regular attendee at county fairs, but I would imagine she's been to street fairs like San Gennaro which feature carnival games.)

@Chris

Agreed the cluing was terrible (terribly easy).  It made the puzzle pointless.  Oddly Butts was a piece of trivia I vaguely recognized.

There were no Naticks.

@Chris

Sometimes, when a piece of trivia is unknowable (unless you're extremely lucky), the point is to suss it out using the gentle crosses provided, and if you can't come up with ESAU or LAMBDA, hopefully you can reason out a fairly common last name based on the rest of the letters you have.

Crosswords are not "Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?"  You have two chances on every square, plus one more if you come close enough to take a non-random guess.

@Rob E

I don't believe there's another set of twins in the book of Genesis; in fact, probably not in the entire Old Testament.  

Come to think of it, I'm unaware of any in the New Testament, either, but I'll admit that most of what I know about it comes from "Jesus Christ Superstar."

